{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>היליכי.</b> Like היניקי, for there are two roots: ילך and הלך, and the meaning is one. For the heh is not of the letters of continuation; therefore a different heh like it [is needed]. Moreover, alef and also yod and vav are found within the word: sometimes written, sometimes quiescent — present in pronunciation, not in writing — and sometimes absent. But the heh is always visible in the middle. And the word בלטיהם (Exodus 7:22) is not like בלהטיהם (Exodus 7:11), as I shall explain." "ותאמר. The Gaon [Saadia] said that היליכי is two words, but he did not speak correctly. Rather, it is one word, on the pattern of והיניקהו לי, as I have said, and it is of the heavy [intensive] construction, the augmented [form]. He also said it is like 'הא לך,' but הא is like וגם אני הא (Ezekiel 16:43). And we have not found in all of Scripture for a feminine ליכי, but only לך. Rather, it is one word." "ותניקהו. With the ayin hidden [quiescent], from the second [binyan] verbs that are visible. Now in one passage there are two roots and the meaning is one. Thus היטיבי נגן (Isaiah 23:16) is imperative, like היליכי. But הטיבו כל אשר דברו (Deuteronomy 5:24) is like והם הביאו אליו (Exodus 36:3), with the ayin hidden."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:07.721440600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}